.TH TOP 1
.SH NAME
top \- monitor processes, CPU time, and memory
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B top
.RB [ \-b ]
.RB [ \-n " count"]
.RB [ \-d " seconds"]
.SH DESCRIPTION
Top displays protected kernel process snapshots. Interactive mode refreshes once
per guest second; q, Ctrl-D, or Ctrl-C exits and restores the terminal. Ctrl-Z
suspends top; fg resumes it. Batch mode prints snapshots without screen control.
Nonterminal input or output defaults to one batch snapshot.
.PP
CPU% is the change in user plus kernel execution ticks divided by elapsed guest
ticks from the host monotonic clock. The first snapshot displays zero. CPU time
samples the selected running task, excluding idle, with at most 100 ms credited
after any polling gap. It estimates guest execution, not host-process CPU usage.
.PP
MAP-K counts mapped data, heap and stack pages. PRIV-K counts pages referenced
only by that process. SHRD-K counts its pages referenced by other processes too.
MAP-K equals PRIV-K plus SHRD-K. Shared columns must not be added across rows;
use free for unique physical page totals. Executable storage and reserved kernel
RAM are outside these per-process counts. Values use physical KiB.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B \-b
Print batch output.
.TP
.BI \-n " count"
Exit after this many snapshots.
.TP
.BI \-d " seconds"
Set the refresh interval (1 through 3276 guest seconds).
.SH SEE ALSO
ps(1), free(1), uptime(1), sh(1)
